# The exact wording of the out-of-range float literal diagnostic.
#
# BEFORE THIS EXISTED, `1e400` was ACCEPTED. strtod returned +HUGE_VAL, codegen's
# E_FLOAT arm ran it through %.17g and emitted the bare token `inf` -- not a C
# keyword, not a standard macro -- so the generated C failed with `'inf'
# undeclared`. The user wrote Tycho and got an error about C, naming a line of a
# file they never wrote. This lane locks the Tycho-level replacement, including
# that the message QUOTES THE LITERAL: an error that says only "out of range"
# leaves the reader hunting for which number in the line it meant.
#
# tests/reject/float_lit_overflow_neg.ty is the companion for the negated form
# (`-1e400`), which reaches the same check because the lexer sees the magnitude
# and the unary minus is a separate token. tests/float_lit_range.ty is the
# positive side: the neighbouring cases that must keep compiling -- underflow to
# zero, DBL_MAX, an inexact finite, and the 1.0/0.0 route to an infinity that
# docs/spec/03-types.md 5.2.2 guarantees and this diagnostic deliberately leaves
# alone.
fn main():
    x := 1e400
    println(str(x))
